{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q1, the consequence that is easiest to get wrong: persistence must NOT re-fire the whole `\"start\"` cohort when a later handler registers. `\"start\"` is already being delivered in a batch run, so each handler fires immediately on its own registration (spec/interaction-events.md:75-77) -- and the run's single `\"start\"` occurrence is what each handler catches as it registers, so handlers registered earlier already caught it. Two byte-identical registrations therefore produce exactly TWO firings, in registration order: register-1 (seq 0-1), fire-1 (seq 2-4), register-2 (seq 5-6), fire-2 (seq 7-9). The two handler-start `instruction` spans differ -- 1:1-1:5 at seq 2, 2:1-2:5 at seq 7 -- which is what discriminates the three readings a persistent `when` admits. Measured against mutated runtimes: firing the cohort on each registration yields THREE prints (a; then a, a), and collapsing the two identical registrations into one handler yields ONE, which is separately forbidden by spec/interaction-events.md:79 (`implementations MUST NOT collapse, deduplicate, or replace registrations, so a block that registers the same handler twice registers two handlers`). Only two prints, at those two spans, is conformant.",
